## Local changes

**1. `hooks/ensure-statusline.mjs`: opt-in recovery trigger.**
Upstream rewrites user `settings.json` at every `SessionStart`. The repository
already evaluated hooks affecting machine state each session:
`multi-ai-delegate/hooks/council_state.py` was not inherited because of session
locking risk, and the session-and-git automatic commit hook was opt-out.
Here the hook does nothing unless the user enabled automatic recovery for that
config directory (a `statusline-repair.json` record in the plugin data directory,
written by setup or `install-statusline.mjs optin`) or set
`JSTACK_DASHBOARD_STATUSLINE=1`; `=0` disables it. When enabled it calls the
installer's idempotent `repair`, so a healthy configuration is neither written nor
logged. The hook derives the payload root from its own file location, because
`CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT` names the package root one level above `payload/`. Its
retained pinned-path match accepts any marketplace and plugin name.

**2. `scripts/statusline-shim.mjs`: installation resolved from host metadata.**
Upstream looks for the fixed identity
`cache/claude-dashboard/claude-dashboard/<version>/dist/index.js`. An earlier port
fixed its own marketplace and plugin name instead, and every render resolved nothing
when the package was installed under another marketplace name. The shim now reads
`plugins/installed_plugins.json`, prefers the installation whose data directory it
sits in, and locates the build beneath each recorded `installPath`; only without a
usable metadata entry does it scan `cache/<marketplace>/<plugin>/<version>/`, a
bounded depth. The only fixed strings are the build locations inside the package
(`DIST_SUBPATHS`: `payload/skills/dashboard/dist/index.js`, and the earlier layout
without `payload/`) and the `JSTACK-DASHBOARD-SHIM` ownership marker.

**3. `scripts/build.js`: remove upstream manifest synchronization.**
That step rewrites the two excluded manifests. Keeping it would produce both
bundles and then exit 1 because the files are missing: **successful artifacts with
a failed exit status**, preventing exit-status enforcement of reproducibility.

**4. Commands: `dashboard.md` renamed to `dashboard-setup.md`, installation by script.**
Upstream's setup command pasted a node snippet that scanned the plugin cache. The
snippets accepted only `X.Y.Z` versions and omitted `payload/`, so nothing installed
under a staging version. They now call `scripts/install-statusline.mjs` (setup,
repair) or `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}` (check-usage). The shell alias, which runs outside
Claude Code, reads the active owned shim from settings and delegates its usage mode;
it no longer scans a fixed marketplace or plugin cache path. The rename is required
because a command
named like the `dashboard` skill is hidden behind the skill. Setup also offers the
emoji-free layout first and lists the new prompt-cache widgets in the detailed mode.

**5. `scripts/widgets/rate-limit.ts`: text marker instead of the warning emoji.**
Upstream renders `ICON.warning` when limit data cannot be read. That branch is the
only emoji an otherwise emoji-free layout can produce, and organization policy
forbids emoji in output. The unreadable state now keeps its label and is named in
text: `5h: --`. The upstream vitest case `should render a text marker when API
failed` follows the same expectation. The source and test are declared as local
changes and leave `UPSTREAM.sha256`. The rebuilt `dist/index.js` also includes
this adaptation and is excluded from the hash list.

**6. `scripts/install-statusline.mjs`: installation, health, and repair (locally authored).**
It resolves the build with the shim's resolver, copies the shim, confirms through the
installed copy which build a render loads, and only then writes `statusLine`, with a
backup and an atomic rename, keeping mode and indentation. It classifies the current
value as `ours`, `empty`, `known-managed-by-another-tool`, or `user`; repair never
replaces `user`, replaces another tool's value only for an identity the user allowed,
and defers on unparsable files. It reports cause types with evidence, can check
several config directories, can keep user-selected sibling snapshots in sync, offers
a foreground watcher with no required OS service, and adds a missing Codex
`tui.status_line` without changing an existing layout.

**7. Status-line vitest cases: jstack package layout and installation identity.**
`scripts/__tests__/statusline-shim.test.ts` and
`scripts/__tests__/ensure-statusline.test.ts` now exercise the packaged dashboard
build path, an installation identity supplied by fixture metadata, and the
opt-in SessionStart hook. Upstream fixtures assumed its standalone plugin cache
and command paths, so those two files are locally modified and excluded from
the unchanged-file hash list.

**8. `vitest.config.ts`: exclude obsolete standalone command-document cases.**
The unchanged upstream `commands-config-dir.test.ts` reads `commands/setup.md`,
`commands/update.md`, `commands/setup-alias.md`, and `README.md`. jstack deliberately
does not ship those documents. Its owning dashboard contract instead executes the
jstack installer, checks the packaged commands for stale paths, and exercises the
SessionStart hook. The upstream test remains in the import for provenance but is
excluded from the applicable Vitest run; the configuration is locally modified and
excluded from the unchanged-file hash list.

**9. `scripts/widgets/prompt-cache.ts`: text markers instead of the new state emojis.**
v1.34.0 added the warm-state Hot Springs glyph and cold-state Snowflake glyph. The
widget and its documentation examples now use the `warm` and `cold` text markers,
and its upstream widget tests assert those strings. The two glyph constants remain
byte-identical to upstream in `scripts/utils/emoji.ts`: the widget does not use them,
so retaining them preserves unchanged-file hash coverage without affecting output.
This source file and `scripts/__tests__/widgets.test.ts` are excluded from
